<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<!--
    The catalogue row skeleton, shared by the product / category / modifier-option / ingredient
    lists. It used to be copy-pasted five times (Border → Grid → content + long press + overflow
    button), and the overflow mark itself a sixth time inside every copy. Declaring the structure
    once here is what let the two x:Reference binding paths per row go away.

    How the commands get in: they are bindable properties, set from the CollectionView's
    DataTemplate on the page (e.g. TapCommand / LongPressCommand / OverflowCommand bound to the
    page ViewModel through the {x:Reference CatalogPage} anchor). They are read back here with
    {Binding X, Source={x:Reference Row}} — a forward reference inside the control's own namescope.
    That direction is safe; the failure mode documented in the codebase is the reverse one, where
    a ContentView tried to look *up* to its host through the ContentView → DataTemplate boundary.

    Three members this file used to name do not exist in .NET MAUI, and naming them was a runtime
    process abort rather than a build error (Debug builds parse XAML at runtime, and a property or
    element name the type does not have is only rejected by ApplyPropertiesVisitor.SetPropertyValue,
    at inflation time):
      * LongPressGestureRecognizer — a WinUI type. MAUI ships no long press at all; the
        CommunityToolkit TouchBehavior already in the project is the equivalent, and it is the
        behaviour (not a gesture recognizer) so it does not compete with the enclosing
        CollectionView's own selection handling the way a TapGestureRecognizer would.
      * Button.Content — MAUI's Button is text-and-image only; it has no content property, and no
        ControlTemplate either (that belongs to TemplatedView), which is why the overflow mark
        cannot come from a style either. The mark is therefore a sibling of the button, not its
        content: the 48dp Grid is the button's own touch target and the Path sits under it.
      * A shapes:Path with no Data draws nothing, so the mark carried its geometry nowhere. It is
        a literal attribute now: a string literal is run through the geometry TypeConverter, which
        is what makes Path="M …" work at all.

    x:DataType
    The DataTemplate this control is declared in decides the BindingContext — Product, Category,
    ModifierOption, ModifierOptionGroup or Ingredient, five different types — so there is no honest
    page-level DataType for the root. Every binding below is a Source binding whose source is the
    row itself, so the type that matters for compilation is CatalogRow, and that is what is
    declared. Nothing in this file binds against the BindingContext, so the declaration is a
    compile-time contract only: it lets XamlC type-check LongPressCommand / OverflowCommand /
    CommandParameter / RowContent / OverflowDescription / IsHeader instead of leaving all six to
    reflection. Point it at any other type and the six bindings silently stop being checked.
-->
<ContentView xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:CafePosApp.Controls"
             xmlns:shapes="clr-namespace:Microsoft.Maui.Controls.Shapes;assembly=Microsoft.Maui.Controls"
             xmlns:toolkit="clr-namespace:CommunityToolkit.Maui.Behaviors;assembly=CommunityToolkit.Maui"
             x:Class="CafePosApp.Controls.CatalogRow"
             x:DataType="controls:CatalogRow"
             x:Name="Row">
    <Border Style="{StaticResource CardBorderCompact}">
        <Border.Triggers>
            <!-- The modifier-group group header sits on a filled Primary bar instead of a card. -->
            <DataTrigger TargetType="Border" Binding="{Binding IsHeader, Source={x:Reference Row}}" Value="True">
                <Setter Property="BackgroundColor" Value="{StaticResource Primary}" />
                <Setter Property="StrokeThickness" Value="0" />
                <Setter Property="Stroke" Value="Transparent" />
                <Setter Property="Padding" Value="10,6" />
            </DataTrigger>
        </Border.Triggers>
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="*,Auto" ColumnSpacing="8">
            <!-- The content column is the long-press target: a long press opens the same sheet as
                 the overflow button.

                 Only LongPressCommand is wired, so a short tap does nothing here and falls
                 through to the list, which drives tap-to-edit from the enclosing CollectionView's
                 SelectionChanged event.

                 There is deliberately no ShouldCaptureTaps="False" on this: that property no
                 longer exists. TouchBehavior became a PlatformBehavior, which observes pointer
                 input instead of consuming it, and the capture opt-out was dropped with it —
                 XamlC fails the build with XC0009 on the attribute, and in a Debug build (which
                 parses XAML at runtime) the unknown property is rejected at inflation, taking
                 every catalogue list on the page with it. -->
            <VerticalStackLayout Grid.Column="0" Spacing="4">
                <VerticalStackLayout.Behaviors>
                    <toolkit:TouchBehavior
                        LongPressCommand="{Binding LongPressCommand, Source={x:Reference Row}}"
                        LongPressCommandParameter="{Binding CommandParameter, Source={x:Reference Row}}"
                        ShouldMakeChildrenInputTransparent="False" />
                </VerticalStackLayout.Behaviors>
                <ContentView Content="{Binding RowContent, Source={x:Reference Row}}" />
            </VerticalStackLayout>
            <!-- The button is a sibling of the mark, not its parent: a MAUI Button has no content
                 property, so <Button.Content> is not markup MAUI can parse. The Grid is sized by
                 the button's own 48dp, the Path is centred under it and takes no input, and the
                 header state is mirrored onto the Path's Fill by the trigger below. -->
            <Grid Grid.Column="1" WidthRequest="48" HeightRequest="48" VerticalOptions="Center">
                <shapes:Path Aspect="Uniform" WidthRequest="24" HeightRequest="24"
                            HorizontalOptions="Center" VerticalOptions="Center"
                            InputTransparent="True"
                            Stroke="Transparent" StrokeThickness="0"
                            Fill="{AppThemeBinding Light={StaticResource Gray500}, Dark={StaticResource Gray400}}"
                            Data="M12,8c1.1,0 2,-0.9 2,-2s-0.9,-2 -2,-2 -2,0.9 -2,2 0.9,2 2,2zM12,10c-1.1,0 -2,0.9 -2,2s0.9,2 2,2 2,-0.9 2,-2 -0.9,-2 -2,-2zM12,16c-1.1,0 -2,0.9 -2,2s0.9,2 2,2 2,-0.9 2,-2 -0.9,-2 -2,-2z">
                    <shapes:Path.Triggers>
                        <DataTrigger TargetType="shapes:Path" Binding="{Binding IsHeader, Source={x:Reference Row}}" Value="True">
                            <Setter Property="Fill" Value="White" />
                        </DataTrigger>
                    </shapes:Path.Triggers>
                </shapes:Path>
                <Button Style="{StaticResource RowOverflowButton}"
                        Command="{Binding OverflowCommand, Source={x:Reference Row}}"
                        CommandParameter="{Binding CommandParameter, Source={x:Reference Row}}"
                        SemanticProperties.Description="{Binding OverflowDescription, Source={x:Reference Row}}">
                    <Button.Triggers>
                        <DataTrigger TargetType="Button" Binding="{Binding IsHeader, Source={x:Reference Row}}" Value="True">
                            <Setter Property="TextColor" Value="White" />
                        </DataTrigger>
                    </Button.Triggers>
                </Button>
            </Grid>
        </Grid>
    </Border>
</ContentView>
